package sqlite

// Decimal <-> binary floating-point conversion with the rounding SQLite 3.53
// and later produce, so reals print and parse exactly as they do there.
//
// Both directions scale by a 96-bit approximation of a power of ten: the
// 64-bit high part `FP_P10_HI[p + 348]` and the next 32 bits
// `FP_P10_LO[p + 348]`, normalized so the top bit is set. For 0 <= p <= 26
// the value is exact; 10^-1 is truncated; multiples of 27 are rounded to 96
// bits; every other power is the 160-bit product of its multiple-of-27
// neighbour below and the exact 10^(p mod 27), truncated to 96 bits (shifted
// left one place, with the lowest bit set, when the product lost its top
// bit). Those truncations are observable in the last digit SQLite prints, so
// the table reproduces them rather than holding exact values; it is generated
// with exact integer arithmetic.
//
// Binary to decimal (`fp_decode`) multiplies the 64-bit significand by the
// high 64 bits of a power of ten and truncates to the requested number of
// digits plus one (18 digits, the last rounded half up, when 17 or more are
// asked for), then rounds half up on that extra decimal digit. Decimal to
// binary (`fp_atof`) keeps the first 19 significant digits of the text in a
// u64 and rounds the scaled product to nearest-even with a sticky bit.

